      "text": "Let's denote the two-digit integer \\( n \\) as \\( 10a + b \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are its digits, with \\( a \\) being the tens digit and \\( b \\) being the units digit. When we switch the digits, the number becomes \\( 10b + a \\).\n\nAccording to the problem, switching the digits and then adding 3 results in \\( 2n \\). So we can write the equation:\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 = 2(10a + b)\n\\]\nExpanding and simplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 = 20a + 2b\n\\]\nRearranging the terms to isolate \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) on one side, we have:\n\\[\n10b + a + 3 - 2b = 20a\n\\]\n\\[\n8b + a + 3 = 20a\n\\]\nSubtracting \\( a \\) from both sides, we get:\n\\[\n8b + 3 = 19a\n\\]\nThis equation tells us that \\( 8b + 3 \\) must be divisible by 19. We need to find values of \\( b \\) (where \\( b \\) is a digit, so \\( 1 \\leq b \\leq 9 \\)) that satisfy this condition. Let's check each possible value of \\( b \\):\n\n1. If \\( b = 1 \\), then \\( 8(1) + 3 = 11 \\), which is not divisible by 19.\n2. If \\( b = 2 \\), then \\( 8(2) + 3 = 19 \\), which is divisible by 19.\n   So, \\( a = \\frac{19}{19} = 1 \\).\n   Therefore, \\( n = 10a + b = 10(1) + 2 = 12 \\).\n\nWe should verify that \\( n = 12 \\) satisfies the original condition:\n- Switching the digits of 12 gives 21.\n- Adding 3 to 21 gives 24.\n- Twice 12 is \\( 2 \\times 12 = 24 \\).\nSince 24 equals 24, the solution is verified.\n\nThus, the smallest two-digit integer \\( n \\) that satisfies the condition is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
