      "text": "Let $\\overline{ab}$ represent the two-digit integer, where $a$ and $b$ are its digits.\n\nAccording to the problem, we have the equation:\n$$\\overline{ba} + 3 = 2 \\times \\overline{ab}$$\nwhere $\\overline{ba}$ represents the number $10b + a$ obtained by switching the digits.\n\nRearranging, we obtain:\n$$100 \\times 10b + 10 \\times 10a + a + 3 = 2 \\times (10a + b)$$\nSimplify further:\n$$(10b + 1)a + 97 = 20a + 2b$$\nNow, we need to find the smallest two-digit integer $n$ that satisfies the equation.\n\nSince $n = 10a + b$, we can substitute $n$ into the equation:\n$$(10b + 1)a + 97 = 20a + 2b$$\n\nSolve for $a$ and $b$ to find the smallest possible value of $n$.\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get:\n\\[a = 8\\]\n\\[b = 3\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest two-digit integer $n$ that satisfies the given condition is:\n$$n = 10a + b = 10(8) + 3 = \\boxed{83}$$",
