      "text": "To solve the problem, let's denote the two-digit number \\( n \\) as \\( 10a + b \\), where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the digits of \\( n \\) with \\( a \\) being the tens digit and \\( b \\) being the units digit. Switching the digits of \\( n \\) means we get the number \\( 10b + a \\). According to the problem, switching the digits and then adding 3 should result in \\( 2n \\). Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ 10b + a + 3 = 2(10a + b) \\]\n\nFirst, expand the right side of the equation:\n\n\\[ 10b + a + 3 = 20a + 2b \\]\n\nNext, we rearrange the terms to isolate the variables on one side of the equation:\n\n\\[ 10b + a + 3 - 2b - 20a = 0 \\]\n\\[ 8b - 19a + 3 = 0 \\]\n\\[ 8b = 19a - 3 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{19a - 3}{8} \\]\n\nSince \\( b \\) is a digit (i.e., \\( 0 \\leq b \\leq 9 \\)), \\( 19a - 3 \\) must be divisible by 8. We need to find an integer \\( a \\) such that \\( 19a - 3 \\) is between 0 and 72 (since the maximum value of \\( b \\) is 9). We will test the values of \\( a \\) from 1 to 4 (since \\( a = 5 \\) and above would make \\( 19a - 3 \\) too large for \\( b \\) to be a digit).\n\n1. If \\( a = 1 \\):\n   \\[ 19 \\cdot 1 - 3 = 16 \\]\n   \\[ b = \\frac{16}{8} = 2 \\]\n   So, \\( n = 10 \\cdot 1 + 2 = 12 \\).\n\n2. If \\( a = 2 \\):\n   \\[ 19 \\cdot 2 - 3 = 35 \\]\n   \\[ b = \\frac{35}{8} = 4.375 \\]\n   Since \\( b \\) is not an integer, \\( a = 2 \\) is not a solution.\n\n3. If \\( a = 3 \\):\n   \\[ 19 \\cdot 3 - 3 = 54 \\]\n   \\[ b = \\frac{54}{8} = 6.75 \\]\n   Since \\( b \\) is not an integer, \\( a = 3 \\) is not a solution.\n\n4. If \\( a = 4 \\):\n   \\[ 19 \\cdot 4 - 3 = 75 \\]\n   \\[ b = \\frac{75}{8} = 9.375 \\]\n   Since \\( b \\) is not an integer, \\( a = 4 \\) is not a solution.\n\nSince the only valid integer solution is for \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 2 \\), the smallest two-digit integer \\( n \\) that satisfies the condition is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\]",
